course: dictionary
lesson: 1524
title: Centurion: Roman, Bible or cricket?
level: primary-5
stars: 3
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and context tells you which one is meant. Sophie in Bristol writes, «Gurt lush, but why's the centurion wearing a wristwatch?» The film is about Rome and the watch is a joke, so a centurion must be a Roman soldier.
explain: In the Bible, centurions appear in the New Testament, such as the centurion at Capernaum whose servant Jesus healed. Read the clues around the word: if the story is about faith or the cross, the centurion is a person from the Bible.
explain: Look at the clues again: «the one called the Centurion is about a hundred metres tall, as tall as a thirty-storey building.» Here it is the name of a tree in Tasmania, not a person. Try your meaning in the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
fact: A centurion was an officer in the army of ancient Rome.
fact: A century was a group of about 80 to 100 soldiers, and a centurion was in charge of it.
fact: In Roman history lessons, centurions had a helmet with a sideways crest and carried a vine stick.
fact: In the Bible, the centurion's faith is a well-known story: «Lord, I am not worthy…»
fact: In cricket slang, a centurion is a player who scores a hundred.
exercise: «Gurt lush, but why's the centurion wearing a wristwatch?» Sophie says this about a Roman film. What is a centurion in this sentence? | *A Roman officer in charge of soldiers | A tall tree in Tasmania | A cricket player who scores a hundred | A kind of wristwatch
exercise: In cricket slang, what is a centurion? | A person who builds walls | An officer in the army of ancient Rome | *A player who scores a hundred
exercise: A century is a group of about how many soldiers? | 5 to 6 | *80 to 100 | 8 to 10 | 800 to 1000
test: In the Bible, what happened to the centurion's servant at Capernaum? | He was sent to Tasmania | He won a cricket match | He became a Roman emperor | *He was healed by Jesus
test: «The one called the Centurion is about a hundred metres tall.» What kind of thing is the Centurion here? | A wristwatch | A soldier in a Roman army | *A tall mountain ash tree | A cricket player
exam: Ellie reads «The centurion at the cross watched closely.» Which meaning fits best? | *A Roman officer in charge of soldiers | A kind of helmet | A cricket player who scores a hundred | A tree in Tasmania
